{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_6","statement":"Childhood Hodgkin lymphoma is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the lymph system.","source_span":"Childhood Hodgkin lymphoma is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the lymph system."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_6","statement":"There are two types of childhood Hodgkin lymphoma: Classical Hodgkin lymphoma and Nodular lymphocyte-predominant Hodgkin lymphoma.","source_span":"The two types of childhood Hodgkin lymphoma are: - Classical Hodgkin lymphoma. - Nodular lymphocyte-predominant Hodgkin lymphoma."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_6","statement":"Classical Hodgkin lymphoma is divided into four subtypes: lymphocyte-rich, nodular sclerosis, mixed cellularity, and lymphocyte-depleted.","source_span":"Classical Hodgkin lymphoma is divided into four subtypes, based on how the cancer cells look under a microscope: - Lymphocyte-rich classical Hodgkin lymphoma. - Nodular sclerosis Hodgkin lymphoma. - Mixed cellularity Hodgkin lymphoma. - Lymphocyte-depleted Hodgkin lymphoma."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_6","statement":"Hodgkin lymphoma often occurs in adolescents 15 to 19 years of age.","source_span":"Hodgkin lymphoma often occurs in adolescents 15 to 19 years of age."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_6","statement":"Epstein-Barr virus infection increases the risk of childhood Hodgkin lymphoma.","source_span":"Epstein-Barr virus infection increases the risk of childhood Hodgkin lymphoma."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_6","statement":"Risk factors for childhood Hodgkin lymphoma include EBV infection, HIV infection, certain immune system diseases, a personal history of mononucleosis, and having a parent or sibling with a personal history of Hodgkin lymphoma.","source_span":"Risk factors for childhood Hodgkin lymphoma include the following: - Being infected with the Epstein-Barr virus. - Being infected with the human immunodeficiency virus (HIV). - Having certain diseases of the immune system. - Having a personal history of mononucleosis (\"mono\"). - Having a parent or sibling with a personal history of Hodgkin lymphoma."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_6","statement":"Being exposed to common infections in early childhood may decrease the risk of Hodgkin lymphoma in children.","source_span":"Being exposed to common infections in early childhood may decrease the risk of Hodgkin lymphoma in children because of the effect it has on the immune system."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_6","statement":"Signs of childhood Hodgkin lymphoma include swollen lymph nodes, fever, night sweats, and weight loss.","source_span":"Signs of childhood Hodgkin lymphoma include swollen lymph nodes, fever, night sweats, and weight loss."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_6","statement":"Fever, weight loss, and night sweats in childhood Hodgkin lymphoma are called B symptoms.","source_span":"Fever, weight loss, and night sweats are called B symptoms."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_6","statement":"Tests used to diagnose childhood Hodgkin lymphoma include physical exam and history, CT scan, PET scan, chest x-ray, and complete blood count (CBC).","source_span":"Physical exam and history ... CT scan (CAT scan) ... PET scan (positron emission tomography scan) ... Chest x-ray ... Complete blood count (CBC)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_6","statement":"In a PET scan, a small amount of radioactive glucose is injected into a vein and malignant tumor cells show up brighter because they take up more glucose than normal cells.","source_span":"A small amount of radioactive glucose (sugar) is injected into a vein. ... Malignant tumor cells show up brighter in the picture because they are more active and take up more glucose than normal cells do."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000004_6","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What are the two main types of childhood Hodgkin lymphoma?","answer":"The two types are classical Hodgkin lymphoma and nodular lymphocyte-predominant Hodgkin lymphoma."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000004_6","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What are the four subtypes of classical Hodgkin lymphoma in children?","answer":"Lymphocyte-rich classical Hodgkin lymphoma, nodular sclerosis Hodgkin lymphoma, mixed cellularity Hodgkin lymphoma, and lymphocyte-depleted Hodgkin lymphoma."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000004_6","fact_refs":[3],"question":"At what age range does Hodgkin lymphoma often occur?","answer":"Hodgkin lymphoma often occurs in adolescents 15 to 19 years of age."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000004_6","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What are the risk factors for childhood Hodgkin lymphoma?","answer":"Risk factors include Epstein-Barr virus infection, HIV infection, certain immune system diseases, a personal history of mononucleosis, and having a parent or sibling with a history of Hodgkin lymphoma."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000004_6","fact_refs":[7,8],"question":"What are the B symptoms of childhood Hodgkin lymphoma?","answer":"Fever, weight loss, and night sweats are called B symptoms; other signs include swollen lymph nodes."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000004_6","fact_refs":[9,10],"question":"How does a PET scan help detect childhood Hodgkin lymphoma?","answer":"A small amount of radioactive glucose is injected into a vein, and the PET scanner shows malignant tumor cells brighter because they take up more glucose than normal cells."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_7","statement":"Childhood non-Hodgkin lymphoma is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the lymph system.","source_span":"Childhood non-Hodgkin lymphoma is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the lymph system."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_7","statement":"There are three major types of childhood non-Hodgkin lymphoma: mature B-cell non-Hodgkin lymphoma, lymphoblastic lymphoma, and anaplastic large cell lymphoma.","source_span":"There are three major types of childhood non-Hodgkin lymphoma. - Mature B-cell non-Hodgkin lymphoma - Lymphoblastic lymphoma - Anaplastic large cell lymphoma"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_7","statement":"Mature B-cell non-Hodgkin lymphomas include Burkitt and Burkitt-like lymphoma/leukemia, diffuse large B-cell lymphoma, and primary mediastinal B-cell lymphoma.","source_span":"Mature B-cell non-Hodgkin lymphomas include: - Burkitt and Burkitt-like lymphoma/leukemia ... - Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma ... - Primary mediastinal B-cell lymphoma"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_7","statement":"Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma is the most common type of non-Hodgkin lymphoma and occurs more often in adolescents than in children.","source_span":"Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma is the most common type of non-Hodgkin lymphoma. ... Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma occurs more often in adolescents than in children."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_7","statement":"Burkitt and Burkitt lymphoma/leukemia have been linked to Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) infection, which is more likely to occur in patients in Africa than in the United States.","source_span":"Both Burkitt leukemia and Burkitt lymphoma have been linked to infection with the Epstein-Barr virus (EBV), although EBV infection is more likely to occur in patients in Africa than in the United States."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_7","statement":"Burkitt and Burkitt-like lymphoma/leukemia are diagnosed when a sample of tissue is checked and a certain change to the c-myc gene is found.","source_span":"Burkitt and Burkitt-like lymphoma/leukemia are diagnosed when a sample of tissue is checked and a certain change to the c-myc gene is found."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_7","statement":"Lymphoblastic lymphoma mainly affects T-cell lymphocytes, usually forms in the mediastinum, and causes trouble breathing, wheezing, trouble swallowing, or swelling of the head and neck.","source_span":"Lymphoblastic lymphoma is a type of lymphoma that mainly affects T-cell lymphocytes. It usually forms in the mediastinum (the area behind the breastbone). This causes trouble breathing, wheezing, trouble swallowing, or swelling of the head and neck."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_7","statement":"Patients with anaplastic large cell lymphoma have a receptor called CD30 on the surface of their T cells.","source_span":"Patients with anaplastic large cell lymphoma have a receptor, called CD30, on the surface of their T cells."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_7","statement":"Past treatment for cancer and having a weakened immune system affect the risk of having childhood non-Hodgkin lymphoma.","source_span":"Past treatment for cancer and having a weakened immune system affect the risk of having childhood non-Hodgkin lymphoma."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_7","statement":"Signs of childhood non-Hodgkin lymphoma include breathing problems and swollen lymph nodes.","source_span":"Signs of childhood non-Hodgkin lymphoma include breathing problems and swollen lymph nodes."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000004_7","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What are the three major types of childhood non-Hodgkin lymphoma?","answer":"The three major types are mature B-cell non-Hodgkin lymphoma, lymphoblastic lymphoma, and anaplastic large cell lymphoma."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000004_7","fact_refs":[3],"question":"Which type of non-Hodgkin lymphoma is most common, and in which age group does it occur more often?","answer":"Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma is the most common type of non-Hodgkin lymphoma, and it occurs more often in adolescents than in children."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000004_7","fact_refs":[4],"question":"Is Burkitt lymphoma/leukemia linked to a virus, and where is that infection more common?","answer":"Yes, it has been linked to Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) infection, which is more likely to occur in patients in Africa than in the United States."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000004_7","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What gene change is used to diagnose Burkitt and Burkitt-like lymphoma/leukemia?","answer":"A certain change to the c-myc gene, found by checking a tissue sample, is used to diagnose Burkitt and Burkitt-like lymphoma/leukemia."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000004_7","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What symptoms can lymphoblastic lymphoma cause and why?","answer":"Because it usually forms in the mediastinum, lymphoblastic lymphoma can cause trouble breathing, wheezing, trouble swallowing, or swelling of the head and neck."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000004_7","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What receptor is present on the T cells of children with anaplastic large cell lymphoma?",	"answer":"They have a receptor called CD30 on the surface of their T cells."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000004_7","fact_refs":[8],"question":"What factors affect the risk of developing childhood non-Hodgkin lymphoma?","answer":"Past treatment for cancer and having a weakened immune system affect the risk of childhood non-Hodgkin lymphoma."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000005_1","statement":"Anal cancer is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the tissues of the anus.","source_span":"Anal cancer is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the tissues of the anus."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000005_1","statement":"Squamous cell carcinoma is the most common type of anal cancer in the United States.","source_span":"In the United States, the most common type of anal cancer is squamous cell carcinoma."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000005_1","statement":"Human papillomavirus (HPV) infection is the main cause of squamous cell carcinoma of the anus.","source_span":"Studies show that human papillomavirus (HPV) infection is the main cause of this type of anal cancer."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000005_1","statement":"From 2004 to 2013, new cases of and deaths from anal cancer increased each year in the United States; the increase in new cases was slightly higher in women and the increase in deaths was slightly higher in men.","source_span":"From 2004 to 2013, new cases of anal cancer and deaths from anal cancer increased each year. The increase in new cases was slightly higher in women and the increase in deaths from anal cancer was slightly higher in men."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000005_1","statement":"Risk factors for anal cancer include HPV infection, having many sexual partners, receptive anal intercourse, being older than 50 years, frequent anal redness/swelling/soreness, anal fistulas, and smoking cigarettes.","source_span":"Risk factors include the following: - Being infected with human papillomavirus (HPV). - Having many sexual partners. - Having receptive anal intercourse (anal sex). - Being older than 50 years. - Frequent anal redness, swelling, and soreness. - Having anal fistulas (abnormal openings). - Smoking cigarettes."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000005_1","statement":"Signs of anal cancer include bleeding from the anus or rectum or a lump near the anus.","source_span":"Signs of anal cancer include bleeding from the anus or rectum or a lump near the anus."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000005_1","statement":"Tests used to diagnose anal cancer include physical exam and history, digital rectal examination (DRE), anoscopy, proctoscopy, endo-anal or endorectal ultrasound, and biopsy.","source_span":"Physical exam and history ... Digital rectal examination (DRE) ... Anoscopy ... Proctoscopy ... Endo-anal or endorectal ultrasound ... Biopsy"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000005_1","statement":"The stages used for anal cancer are Stage 0 (Carcinoma in Situ), Stage I, Stage II, Stage IIIA, Stage IIIB, and Stage IV.","source_span":"The following stages are used for anal cancer: - Stage 0 (Carcinoma in Situ) - Stage I - Stage II - Stage IIIA - Stage IIIB - Stage IV"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000005_1","fact_refs":[1,2],"question":"What is the most common type of anal cancer in the United States, and what is its main cause?","answer":"Squamous cell carcinoma is the most common type of anal cancer in the United States, and it is mainly caused by human papillomavirus (HPV) infection."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000005_1","fact_refs":[3],"question":"How did anal cancer incidence and mortality trends differ between men and women from 2004 to 2013?","answer":"From 2004 to 2013 both new cases and deaths from anal cancer increased each year; the rise in new cases was slightly higher in women, while the rise in deaths was slightly higher in men."}
